The shaded region lies between $y=x^{5}$ and the $x$-axis, from $x=-2$ to $x=2$.
Find its **area**.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, x=1.2cm, y=0.115cm]
\fill[gray!35, domain=-2:0, variable=\x] (-2,0) -- plot ({\x},{\x*\x*\x*\x*\x}) -- (0,0) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!35, domain=0:2, variable=\x] (0,0) -- plot ({\x},{\x*\x*\x*\x*\x}) -- (2,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-2.9,0) -- (2.9,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-42) -- (0,42) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-2.05:2.05, smooth, samples=140, variable=\x] plot ({\x},{\x*\x*\x*\x*\x});
\node[above left] at (-2,0) {$-2$};
\node[below right] at (2,0) {$2$};
\node[left] at (0,30) {$30$};
\node[left] at (0,-30) {$-30$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Use symmetry to evaluate $\int_{-1}^{1}\left(x^{9}-4x\right)dx$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, x=2cm, y=0.9cm]
\fill[gray!35, domain=-1:0, variable=\x] (-1,0) -- plot ({\x},{\x*\x*\x*\x*\x*\x*\x*\x*\x-4*\x}) -- (0,0) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!35, domain=0:1, variable=\x] (0,0) -- plot ({\x},{\x*\x*\x*\x*\x*\x*\x*\x*\x-4*\x}) -- (1,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-1.6,0) -- (1.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-4.4) -- (0,4.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-1.1:1.1, smooth, samples=160, variable=\x] plot ({\x},{\x*\x*\x*\x*\x*\x*\x*\x*\x-4*\x});
\node[above left] at (-1,0) {$-1$};
\node[below right] at (1,0) {$1$};
\node[left] at (0,3) {$3$};
\node[left] at (0,-3) {$-3$};
